.TH FSYNC 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
fsync \- request that files be flushed to disk
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsync
.RB [ \-d ]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fsync
opens each
.I file
read-only, requests that its buffered data be written to disk, and
closes it, in the order given.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped, and the
remaining files are still processed.
Nothing is written to standard output.
.PP
On Windows, a file opened read-only cannot be flushed.
The request fails with an access error, which
.B fsync
treats as success, so naming a file flushes nothing.
See
.B NOTES
for a way to flush.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-dd ,
and may follow
.I file
operands.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.B \-d
Avoid syncing metadata, by calling
.B fdatasync
instead of
.BR fsync .
This build has no
.B fdatasync
and calls
.B fsync
either way, so
.B \-d
changes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows may show a critical-error dialog for a
.I file
on a drive with no disk in it.
Otherwise busybox suppresses the dialog, and the file fails to open.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is, and fails to open with
.BR "Invalid argument" .
A busybox process started with the variable unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that a later
.B \-d
is a file name.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every
.I file
was opened and processed.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, no
.I file
operand, or a
.I file
that could not be opened or flushed.
.PP
A usage message for an invalid option or a missing operand is written to
standard error.
A file that cannot be opened is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fsync: can\(aqt open \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
and one that cannot be flushed as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fsync: \fIfile\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Flush a file through a handle that the shell opens for appending, which,
unlike one that
.B fsync
opens itself, has write access:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ fsync /dev/stdout >>data.db
.EE
.RE
.PP
A file that cannot be opened is reported, and the exit status is 1:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ fsync data.db missing.db; echo $?
fsync: can\(aqt open \(aqmissing.db\(aq: No such file or directory
1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy a file and flush the copy.
Unlike
.BR fsync ,
.B dd
flushes through the handle it wrote with:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dd if=in.bin of=out.bin conv=fsync
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
For a named file, opening it for reading is all that
.B fsync
does.
Windows allows
.B FlushFileBuffers
only on a handle with write access, and
.B fsync
opens each file read-only.
The flush fails with an access-denied error, which busybox's emulation
of the C function
.B fsync
deliberately reports as success.
The exit status therefore shows only that each file could be opened for
reading.
.PP
To get data to disk, flush through a handle with write access.
.B dd\ conv=fsync
does so for its output file.
.I /dev/stdout
and
.I /dev/fd/N
reuse an open descriptor, with its access rights, so the shell's
redirection in the first example under
.B EXAMPLES
gives
.B fsync
a handle it can flush.
.PP
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The device names
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/tty ,
.IR /dev/zero ,
and
.I /dev/urandom
are accepted, and succeed.
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/stdout ,
.IR /dev/stderr ,
and
.I /dev/fd/N
name open descriptors, and one that is open for writing to a console
fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dd (1),
.BR sync (1)
